-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Revision Lifecycle
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+- The first accepted submission for a visit creates revision 1 and the visit is current.
+- A submission whose base revision ID equals the visit's current revision ID applies cleanly: its payload becomes the next revision, and the visit remains current.
+- A submission whose base revision ID is stale (the visit has since moved to a newer revision) is retained as a **competing revision**. The visit enters the **unresolved** state: the previously current revision stays current, and the competing revision waits for coordinator review.
+- Multiple competing revisions may accumulate against the same current revision; each is retained separately.
+- Resolution ends the unresolved state. The coordinator either accepts a competing revision as-is, which makes it the current revision, or records a corrected payload with the resolution. Either way the outcome becomes a new revision in the chain and the resolved competing revisions are marked resolved — never deleted — so the full path from any submission to an exported value stays inspectable.
+- A base revision ID that names no revision of that visit is rejected as invalid rather than treated as stale.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Current-State Retrieval
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Retrieval distinguishes usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit response carries the current revision's payload and revision ID, a flag indicating whether unresolved review work exists, and — when it does — the list of unresolved competing revisions with their authors, receipt times, and base revision IDs. Unresolved competing revisions are never served as the current state.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Review-Outcome Operations
+
+Resolving review work records the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs, which outcome was chosen (accepted revision or corrected payload), and the resolution time. Resolution is idempotent by resolution ID: repeating it returns the original outcome.
+
+## Tenet Alignment
+
+Preserving every submission — resolved or competing — satisfies the HLD tenets that no submitted edit is silently discarded and that the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stays inspectable. The cost, accepted deliberately, is review volume: unrelated-field edits between two workers produce competing revisions that a coordinator must resolve by hand.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope (retained base revision IDs, pending competing revisions). Review-export will size its coordinator interface around the unresolved-work representation and exclude unresolved visits from its export set. Both link back to this node.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent-edit policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves each worker's submitted visit as a scientific unit; avoids maintained merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits. Trade-off accepted: higher coordinator review volume. Full trade analysis: docs/intent/record-store/decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md |
+| Stale-base edits | Retain as competing revisions pending review | Reject stale submissions; auto-merge fields | Rejecting would force workers to re-edit after losing their base, punishing offline work; auto-merge was rejected per the policy decision above. |
+| Resolution outcomes | Accept a competing revision as-is, or record a corrected payload | Force selection among competing revisions only | A coordinator comparing revisions may see that both miss something; a corrected payload keeps the resolution honest without introducing server merge logic. |
+| Competing revisions after resolution | Marked resolved, retained | Delete superseded revisions | Keeps the path from any submitted edit to the exported value inspectable, per HLD tenets. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether two competing revisions with payload-identical content to the current revision auto-resolve or still require coordinator review.
+2. Whether resolution is permitted while further competing revisions are still arriving, or waits for a quiet window.
